# Stations for ~/.local/bin/focus-music. One per line: a name, a TAB, a target.
#
# A target is either a URL, played directly, or the word search: followed by
# text, which is handed to YouTube and resolved to whatever it finds now. The
# picker also offers an ad-hoc search, and anything kept from one is written as
# the second kind - see WHAT IS KEPT IS A SEARCH, NOT A LINK in the helper.
# `focus-music --check` resolves every line here and says what no longer answers.
#
# Tracked here rather than inside the helper, so adding a station is editing
# data rather than editing a script - and so a rebuilt machine has the same
# list. Comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# THE NAMES LEAD WITH THE SOUND, NOT THE BRAND
#
# "Fluid" and "Lush" tell you nothing at the moment you are choosing, which is
# the only moment the name is read. The descriptor comes first and the station
# name follows it, so the list can be skimmed for a mood and still says where
# each one came from. rofi matches anywhere in the line, so typing "lush" or
# "dub" both still find them.
#
# WHY DIRECT AUDIO URLS
#
# No yt-dlp, no API, no key, and no resolving step that can break independently
# of the player - every URL below was checked returning audio before being
# written down. A YouTube live stream needs resolving on every start and its
# video id changes whenever the channel restarts, which turns a station into a
# thing that stops working for reasons nobody can see.
#
# yt-dlp is still declared, so pasting a YouTube or SoundCloud link here works.
# Exactly one station below is one - see THE ONE EXCEPTION.
#
# THE ONE EXCEPTION
#
# There is no direct audio stream of Minecraft's soundtrack anywhere. The open
# radio directory returns nothing for that tag or that name, and both laut.fm
# stations called "minecraft" are mislabelled - one plays chart pop, the other
# NoCopyrightSounds. So the Minecraft entry below is a YouTube URL, resolved by
# yt-dlp on every start, and it carries the weaknesses this file warns about: a
# few seconds before sound, and silence if yt-dlp goes stale against YouTube.
#
# It is a fixed upload rather than a live stream, and that part is not taste.
# The channel running the obvious "minecraft music 24/7" broadcast recycles its
# video id every time it restarts - its own list carries several dead ids under
# one title - and its /live URL was pointing at an unrelated stream on the day
# this was written, so neither the id nor the channel is a stable thing to name.
# A published video's id does not move. The cost is that it ends after ten
# hours instead of never.
#
# SomaFM is listener-supported and serves no ads: https://somafm.com/support/
# Their channel list, with ids for the URL pattern below, is at
# https://somafm.com/channels.json

